  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x^2 + 0.5 z^2) / 2, drawn at half its value as height. Steep left-right (x), gentle front-back (z). Plain steps in x grow when learning rate > 2/2.4 ≈ 0.83, so swings can grow at the top of the range.",
    "Each step: position -= learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise per axis has spread 0.6 × sqrt((64 − b)/(63 b)) for batch b, like sampling b of 64 points: biggest at 1, exactly zero at 64. Fixed seed, so Reset replays the same noise.",
    "Arrows show minus the slope (not the step), drawn at 0.5 × its size, one scale for both. Purple: mini-batch estimate, the way the ball moves. Dark: full-data (true) slope. Hidden once the run ends.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 5 or the ball leaves the drawn square (diverged). The ball and path are then drawn at the rim; the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The trail holds at most 41 points: the start plus one per step. 'New run, keep last path' keeps the finished run as the ghost and uses the next noise values."
  ]
}